# The settings a run has: one name per line.
#
# The interpreter's dictionaries hold the language -- the same names with
# the same values however the interpreter was started. These are the
# values that are not like that. Each is decided afresh on every launch,
# by the command line, the environment, the caller, or the state of the
# process, and each lives in .hostdict, the one dictionary that holds
# such things and nothing else.
#
# What the register is for is that the distinction stays a structural one
# rather than a judgement made afresh for every name. A value that comes
# from the invocation and is kept anywhere but here is one whose home
# says it is part of the language; a name here that the interpreter does
# not settle is a setting readers can ask for and never get.
# check-host-settings.sh holds the register to the C table that writes
# these, to the readers among the boot files, and to a live interpreter.
#
# The "entries" line counts the names that follow. Without it the list
# could be emptied and go on agreeing with everything asked of it: with
# nothing registered there is nothing to look for.
#
# A name is added here in the same commit that makes the interpreter
# settle it, and removed in the one that stops.

entries 10

# where this run found its boot files: XPOST_DATA_DIR, the directory the
# shared library sits in, the configured data directory, or a relative
# path from wherever the process was started
DATA_DIR

# the directories a resource search covers when findresource misses in
# virtual memory, in the order they are searched: -I, and the calls an
# embedding caller makes to xpost_add_resource_dir
.resourcepath

# whether this run has a user at the other end of it: settled from
# whether the caller asked for a batch run and from whether standard
# input is a terminal, and settled again for every run the context serves
.interactive

# what showpage does when a page ends -- pause, carry on, or hand
# control back to the caller -- as the context was created with
ShowpageSemantics

# which device this run was started with: -d, less the mode selector a
# "device:mode" selection carries. The boot files make the device from
# it once the language stands, and the start-up report names it when the
# device could not be made
StartDevice

# the page this run was started at, in device pixels: -g, or the page a
# run that named none is started at
StartPageSize

# the mode selector of a "device:mode" selection (-d raster:bgra), which
# the raster device reads to settle its pixel format
SUBDEVICE

# where this run's pages go when the page device names nothing and the
# program has bound no name of its own: -o
OutputFileName

# a framebuffer an embedding caller lent this run, and the place such a
# caller wants the finished one written back to: the pointers travel in
# strings a program cannot read
OutputBufferIn
OutputBufferOut
